  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Areas of fog or low clouds again possible tonight.\n\n-  Light precipitation is possible Thursday onward.\n\nLooking at satellite you can clearly define mountains to valleys,\nalmost all valleys lower than 6000 feet show up white or filled\nwith low clouds. The mountains are completely clear. The\npersistent low stratus may linger through late afternoon limiting\nmaximum warming. As an example, the forecast high for Missoula is\n48F today, yet at 12:20 pm we are still 34F. Clearly, getting to\njust 40F will be difficult. \n\nA weak shortwave is expected to break off the main low pressure\nsystem off the coast of British Colombia and move through the\nNorthern Rockies tonight into tomorrow morning. Valley inversions\nare very strong and while southwesterly breezes increase in the\nmountains tonight, another morning of fog and low stratus is\npossible Thursday. A period of light precipitation is expected\nwith the short wave Thursday morning along the ID/MT border. Any \nprecipitation will be light with flurries or light snow showers \nabove 4500 feet. \n\nHigh pressure immediately rebuilds late Friday and patchy valley\nfog or low stratus is possible Saturday. Depending how quickly\nvalley inversions rebuild, Saturday afternoon could be quite warm\nfor this time of year.  \n\nThe Northern Rockies gets clipped by another weak wave Sunday.\nWhile light precipitation is possible in far NW MT, A breezy day\nwith gusts 25 to 35 mph can be expected in the mountains.\n\nHigh pressure yet again rebuilds and as valley inversions return\nto start next week, afternoon highs slowly cool while valley fog \nand low stratus are added to the morning menu.",
